$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{8-3x}{9x^2+11}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[8-3x\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[9x^2+11]}\qquad\gray{\text{l’Hôpital’s rule}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-3}{18x}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{-1}{6x}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$

Note that we were only able to use l’Hôpital’s rule because the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[8-3x\right]}{\dfrac{d}{dx}[9x^2+11]}$ can actually be determined.
